Bash	cat > /tmp/issue12.md <<'EOF'; **What.** When the merchant changes the rate of a partner who has customers, a dialog asks where the new rate applies:; ; - **New customers only** (selected by default): the current customers keep their rate; only customers brought from now on pay the new rate.; - **All customers**: every current customer of the partner pays the new rate from the next payment.; ; The partner's history records the choice.; ; **Why.** The client answered our question 1 of 23 Oct in the letter of 30 Oct (`docs/letters/2026-10-30-client.md`): a new rate applies only to customers brought after the change, and the commissions on current customers stay as they are. On 6 Nov 2026 we decided to make that the default and to keep "All customers" as a choice in the dialog. This replaces the decision of 29 Oct, which made "All customers" the default.; ; **Acceptance.** A partner has 3 customers at 5%. The merchant sets 7% and keeps the default: the 3 customers still pay the partner 5%, and a new customer brought after the change pays 7%. With "All customers", the next payment of each of the 3 customers pays the partner 7%.; EOF; gh issue edit 12 --body-file /tmp/issue12.md && gh issue comment 12 --body "Default changed to \"New customers only\" after the client's letter of 30 Oct; \"All customers\" stays a choice (decided 6 Nov 2026)." && rm /tmp/issue12.md
text	Here is the paragraph for the reply:  > **1. A change of a partner's rate.** Thank you for your answer. A new rate will apply only to customers that the partner brings after the change. The commissions on current customers will stay as they are, and this will be the default. When you change a rate, a short dialog will also give a second option, "All customers". Select it only if you want the current customers of the partner to pay the new rate too, from their next payment. If you do nothing, the current customers keep their rate.  I also updated issue #12 to match your decision: - "New customers only" is now the default. "All customers" is the second option. - The "Why" section now cites the letter of 30 Oct and the decision of 6 Nov. It says that this decision replaces the one of 29 Oct. - I rewrote the acceptance test. With the default, the 3 customers stay at 5%, and a new customer pays 7%. - I left a one-line comment on #12 that says what changed and why.  One point is still open. The client asked to see every rate change in the partner's history. #12 only records the choice made in the dialog. Tell me if you want a separate issue for a full rate history.
